    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504028/manager-curriculum-analytics-and-partnership": {
      "title": "Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Professional Management 1 Manager, Curriculum Analytics and Partnership Apply now Job no: 504028 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart Categories: Management The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Lead a team that transforms data and insights into meaningful improvements Develop your expertise while driving meaningful change across the University Full time, Fixed-Term (September 2027) Hobart Location About the opportunity The University of Tasmania is seeking a strategic and forward-thinking Manager, Curriculum Analytics and Partnerships to play a pivotal role within our Learning and Teaching Performance team. Working closely with the Director, Performance Optimisation, you will lead the development and implementation of innovative analytics tools, performance monitoring frameworks, and digital solutions that support curriculum quality and continuous improvement across the University. You will combine strategic insight with operational leadership to ensure academic leaders can access meaningful data and tools that inform decision-making and strengthen student outcomes. What you’ll do: Lead the development and continuous improvement of curriculum analytics platforms, dashboards, workflows, and monitoring tools. Support evidence-based decision-making through the use of data, emerging technologies, and performance insights. Partner with academic leaders and professional staff to strengthen curriculum quality and institutional performance. Manage key external partnerships, including the University's relationship with Open Universities Australia (OUA). Drive the adoption of curriculum performance frameworks that support quality assurance, governance, and compliance requirements. Provide leadership and guidance within Performance Optimisation, fostering a culture of collaboration, innovation, and continuous improvement. What we’re looking for: Demonstrated experience leading complex projects, programs, or initiatives in a large organisation. Strong analytical capability and experience designing or implementing digital tools, reporting solutions, or performance frameworks. Exceptional stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence and work effectively across diverse groups. The ability to balance strategic thinking with operational delivery. Experience driving change, innovation, and continuous improvement. Relevant tertiary qualifications and/or equivalent professional experience. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 9 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $160,613 comprising base salary within the range of $130,242 to $137,276 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the success profile. For further information about this position, please contact Kate Lee, Director Performance Optimisation, Kate.Lee@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Manager-Curriculum Analytics and Partnership Position Description (504028).pdf Applications close Tuesday, 8 September 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504151/people-and-wellbeing-officer": {
      "title": "Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Professional Human Resources 1 People and Wellbeing Officer Apply now Job no: 504151 Work type: Full time Location: Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Human Resources Join a newly created, high-impact HR services function Deliver trusted advice and support across the employee lifecycle Fixed term and ongoing opportunities available state-wide in Tasmania The University of Tasmania acknowledges the palawa/pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. About the Opportunity We are seeking Human Resource administrators to join our newly formed People Connect team as People and Wellbeing Officers. Established through a strategic redesign of the People and Wellbeing division, People Connect will play a central role in how employees and leaders access support across the University. Providing high-quality advice and operational support across the employee lifecycle, the team is focused on delivering a connected, consistent and responsive people experience. This is an exciting opportunity to be part of something new. As a People & Wellbeing Officer, you will help bring the People Connect vision to life by providing practical HR advice, resolving enquiries and supporting key people processes. Working closely with Business Partners and specialist teams, you will help ensure staff and leaders receive timely, accurate and trusted support. As the service continues to evolve, you will contribute to improving processes, embracing digital tools and supporting greater use of self-service resources. You will play an important role in helping simplify the way we work while enhancing the experience of employees and leaders across the University. Success in this role will require someone who enjoys helping people, brings a customer-focused mindset and can confidently balance service, accuracy and efficiency. You will be adaptable, collaborative and motivated by opportunities to learn, improve and contribute to a positive team culture. What’s Required We are seeking candidates who are passionate about delivering exceptional service and building positive relationships, including: Demonstrated experience providing HR advice and/or operational support within a policy, procedural or regulatory environment. Proven ability to interpret policies, procedures and employment conditions and translate them into practical, solutions-focused advice. Experience working in a service-oriented environment, managing enquiries and delivering outcomes through structured workflows or case management systems. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships across a diverse stakeholder group. Demonstrated ability to deliver accurate and timely outcomes in a high-volume environment. Confidence using systems, workflow tools and digital technologies to support efficient service delivery. The ability to exercise judgement, escalate matters appropriately and work collaboratively with Business Partners and specialist teams. A commitment to continuous improvement, service excellence and finding better ways of working. What’s on Offer? Appointment to this role will be at HEO 5 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $105,665 comprising base salary within the range of $80,869 to $90,312 plus 17% superannuation. This role is available in Hobart, Burnie or Launceston, fulltime, on either a 6 month fixed term or ongoing basis. You’ll also have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and Car Hire discounts Health Insurance and Banking Novated Leasing Belong - Be You People are at the heart of everything we do. Our vision is to ensure that our people feel encouraged and supported to bring their whole selves to work. Fostering diversity and inclusion is our key priority, supporting our communities to come together in ways that empower connection, learning, belonging and growth. Celebrating the strengths that diversity brings will enable us to achieve our vision and mission. Applications are encouraged from all sectors of the community. Tell us how we can make this job work for you. How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Please provide your up-to-date resume. Cover letter (maximum 1 page) outlining your suitability and motivation for the role For further information about this position, please contact Sage Grantham, Manager People Connect via sage.grantham@utas.edu.au Position Description - People & Wellbeing Officer.pdf Applications close Tuesday 8 September 2026, 11:55pm To be eligible for this position, you are required to have Australian citizenship, permanent residence or a current valid visa that allows you to fulfill the requirements of this role. Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes People and Wellbeing Officer Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 8 Sep 2026 Join a newly created, high-impact HR services function, delivering trusted advice and support across the employee lifecycle Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes People and Wellbeing Officer Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 8 Sep 2026 Join a newly created, high-impact HR services function, delivering trusted advice and support across the employee lifecycle Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504154/director-business-partnering": {
      "title": "Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Professional Human Resources 1 Director Business Partnering Apply now Job no: 504154 Work type: Full time Location: Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Human Resources The University of Tasmania acknowledges the palawa/pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present . Shape how leaders navigate complex people and culture challenges. Lead a high-performing team through growth and transformation. Drive trusted, care-informed solutions that strengthen organisational capability. About the Opportunity The University of Tasmania is seeking to appoint an exceptional leader as Director, Business Partnering and Advisory Services. Reporting to the Chief People Officer, you will lead an integrated portfolio spanning business partnering, advisory services, complaints management, and complex case management. Working closely with academic and professional leaders, you will provide strategic advice, build leadership capability, and drive workforce outcomes that support the University's strategic priorities. This role will see you lead a high-performing team, strengthen a contemporary business partnering model, and ensure leaders receive trusted, consistent and timely support across a broad range of people matters. You will also oversee frameworks that enable effective case management, governance, and continuous improvement, ensuring complex matters are managed with care, integrity, and sound judgement. This is an outstanding opportunity to influence culture, leadership, and workforce strategy across a university whose mission is to build a better future for Tasmania through education, research, and connection. What’s Required Demonstrated success in leading and managing HR business partners in a large, diverse and complex organisation, driving cultural change and delivering people-centric services aligned with organisational goals. Demonstrated HR strategy leadership with capacity to link strategic goals with operational decisions, and develop, implement, and execute people and wellbeing strategies, translating the University's mission and strategy into actionable plans that drive organisational effectiveness. Strong relationship building skills with the ability to advice, coach and build mutually beneficial relationships with key stakeholders while setting and resetting expectations at the executive level, addressing uncomfortable truths, and working collaboratively to develop solutions or compromises that resolve complex challenges. Proven capacity to thrive and lead others in a complex and changing environment and lead decisions that have an impact across the University. A demonstrated ability to be 'hands on', curious about the why and focused on outcomes, supported by data driven insights to influence leaders in building an agile University through scale and simplification. Demonstrated experience in refining service models and using KPIs to drive continuous improvement, measure success, enhance accountability, and optimise overall performance A demonstrated passion and experience in shaping a highly engaging employee and team experience, fostering a positive and inclusive culture, and developing individuals' skills and capabilities. What’s on Offer? In addition, you’ll have access an array of staff benefits and discounts including. 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers 17% Superannuation contribution Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and Car Hire discounts Health Insurance and Banking Novated Leasing About the University At the University of Tasmania, we have mission to make a difference for Tasmania, and from Tasmania. With our unique location and complex history, we have both an obligation and opportunity to make a global contribution. People are at the heart of that contribution. We focus on building a workplace culture where we come together in a way that empowers us all to connect, belong and grow. The University is one of the largest employers in our State, with campuses in three distinct regions: Hobart, Launceston, and Burnie. We also have a specialised health campus in Sydney. We strive to stimulate economic growth, lift literacy, improve health outcomes for Tasmania and nurture our environment as it nurtures us. We welcome enquiring minds, from near and far, to join us in our pursuit of the extraordinary. How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Your application must include: Your up-to-date resume A 2-page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role and includes an overview of how your previous skills & experience aligns to the requirements of the role Please submit your application via our PageUp portal For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. For more information about the role please contact Sophie Chandler, Associate Director Careers, via email at sophie.chandler@utas.edu.au Position Description - Director Business Partnering - August 2026.pdf Please note applications close on Sunday 6 th of September at 11.55pm. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 06 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Director Business Partnering Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 6 Sep 2026 Lead a critical people leadership portfolio, enabling confident leadership, strong culture, and exceptional employee experiences across the University. Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Director Business Partnering Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 6 Sep 2026 Lead a critical people leadership portfolio, enabling confident leadership, strong culture, and exceptional employee experiences across the University. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504056/senior-lecturer-in-leadership-health-and-human-services": {
      "title": "Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Academic Teaching Focused 1 Senior Lecturer in Leadership (Health and Human Services) Apply now Job no: 504056 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart Categories: Teaching Focused The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Lead postgraduate leadership education in health and human services Influence curriculum, industry engagement and future leaders Full-time, ongoing role, based in Hobart - part-time and research fraction considered About the opportunity: We are seeking a teaching-focused Senior Lecturer to play a key role in delivering and shaping postgraduate Leadership (Health and Human Services) programs at the Graduate Certificate, Graduate Diploma and Master's levels. Working closely with the Program Lead, the successful candidate will provide outstanding learning experiences for students, contribute to curriculum design and continuous improvement, and lead quality assurance activities to ensure courses meet university and national professional standards. The role also involves collaborating with academic colleagues to deliver innovative, place-based education, with opportunities to coordinate courses and provide leadership and support to academic staff. While primarily teaching-focused, the position may also offer scope for a research component and flexible employment arrangements where appropriate. Accountabilities and Outcomes: Deliver exceptional postgraduate teaching and learning experiences across Leadership (Health and Human Services) programs. Lead the design, coordination and continuous improvement of contemporary, high-quality curriculum and assessment. Drive course quality and innovation through evidence-based improvements informed by student feedback, outcomes and industry developments. Build and maintain strong partnerships with health sector stakeholders, including the Tasmanian Department of Health, to ensure programs remain relevant and impactful. Provide academic leadership and mentorship that supports staff wellbeing, collaboration and excellence in teaching. Role Specific Skills, Knowledge and Experience A PhD or equivalent in a relevant field. Experience and demonstrated excellence in achievement in University-level teaching and learning. A record of contributing effectively to building and maintaining effective and productive links locally and nationally with the discipline, profession, industry (where relevant) and wider community. Substantial evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills, with a record of contributing to building and maintaining effective and productive links locally and nationally with the discipline, profession, industry, and the wider community. Extensive relevant Health and Human Services sector experience. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level C and will have a total remuneration package of up to $184,076 comprising base salary within the range of $138,536 to $157,330 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience. For further information about this position, please contact Professor Nicola Stephens, Director Postgraduate Programs Nicola.Stephens@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - 504056 - Senior Lecturer in Leadership (Health and Human Services).pdf Applications close Monday, 7 September 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 24 Aug 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 07 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Senior Lecturer in Leadership (Health and Human Services) Hobart 7 Sep 2026 Lead the delivery and development of high-quality postgraduate leadership programs that empower professionals to drive positive change across health and human services Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Senior Lecturer in Leadership (Health and Human Services) Hobart 7 Sep 2026 Lead the delivery and development of high-quality postgraduate leadership programs that empower professionals to drive positive change across health and human services Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
