    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504189/lecturer-in-initial-teacher-education": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Initial Teacher Education",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Burnie 1 Hobart 1 Launceston 1 Categories Academic Teaching Intensive 1 Lecturer in Initial Teacher Education Apply now Job no: 504189 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart, Launceston, Burnie Categories: Teaching Intensive The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Inspire future teachers through engaging teaching, curriculum development and research excellence Collaborate with education partners across Tasmania to drive innovation and system-wide impact 3 Full-time, ongoing positions located in Hobart, Launceston or Burnie. Flexible work arrangements available About the opportunity We are seeking to appoint three Lecturers in Initial Teacher Education to contribute to the delivery of contemporary teacher education programs and undertake impactful research within a collaborative academic environment. As a Lecturer in Initial Teacher Education you will be an innovative educator-researcher who contributes to the design, development and delivery of high-quality teaching and curriculum development, while conducting impactful research aligned with the University of Tasmania mission. The successful applicant will design and teach engaging online and face to face learning experiences while contributing to excellence in teacher education, research and engagement. The successful candidate will have experience in primary or secondary teacher education with expertise in at least one of the following areas; Arts, Mathematics, Technologies or Inclusive Education, and will have a strong commitment to the crucial role of education to enable individuals and communities to flourish. Our ambition is to positively impact the wellbeing and educational attainment of all Tasmanians and we see this role as central to this endeavour. In this role, you will engage directly with the Tasmanian Department of Education, Children and Young People, Catholic Education Tasmania, Independent Schools Tasmania as well as early years providers and community organisations to build capacity across the education system in Tasmania. The very direct engagement with these bodies that is possible in Tasmania is a strategic advantage which we anticipate will facilitate system-wide change. What you’ll do: Develop, coordinate and teach high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate teaching programs in initial teacher education. Contribute to innovative curriculum design and learning experiences that support student success and graduate readiness Coordinate and provide academic leadership to support tutorial staff where required. Make an effective and sustained contribution to the School of Education and the University in achieving its strategic objectives and fulfilling its operational responsibilities. Undertake high-quality research of national and increasingly of international standing, in order to meet and regularly exceed the University’s research performance expectations for Level B. ­ What we’re looking for: A PhD in a relevant field or equivalent (for example, a Masters level qualification in a relevant field and leadership experience in the field of teaching, curriculum development or school management). Curriculum leadership experience in primary or secondary schools (e.g. Arts, Mathematics, Technologies) and/or expertise in professional knowledge (eg. inclusive education) Demonstrated record of, and ongoing commitment to, research and writing that has achieved national recognition and made worthwhile contributions to the field of Education Demonstrated experience in delivering and supporting high-quality University level teaching and learning in one or more of the key areas outlined Experience designing or contributing to curriculum and assessment aligned with learning outcomes A record of contributing to building and maintaining effective and productive links locally and nationally with the discipline, profession, industry and wider community. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level B and will have a total remuneration package of up to $155,885 comprising base salary within the range of $114,766 to $133,235 plus 17% superannuation. Relocation support may be available to the successful candidate, helping make a move to Tasmania as seamless as possible. How to Apply To apply online, please provide the following supporting documentation: Resume Cover letter outlining your suitability and motivation for the role Your responses to the Role Specific Skills, Knowledge and Experience from the Position Description below For further information about this position, please contact Professor Louise Starkey, Professor of Education, Arts & Society, Louise.Starkey@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - 504189 - Lecturer in Initial Teacher Education.pdf Applications close Sunday, 4 October 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 04 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Lecturer in Initial Teacher Education Hobart, Launceston, Burnie 4 Oct 2026 Help shape the next generation of teachers through innovative teaching, impactful research, and strong education partnerships Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Lecturer in Initial Teacher Education Hobart, Launceston, Burnie 4 Oct 2026 Help shape the next generation of teachers through innovative teaching, impactful research, and strong education partnerships Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504199/year-3-coordinator-of-general-practice-placements": {
      "title": "Year 3 Coordinator of General Practice Placements",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Part time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Academic Teaching Focused 1 Year 3 Coordinator of General Practice Placements Apply now Job no: 504199 Work type: Part time Location: Hobart Categories: Teaching Focused The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Provide academic and clinical leadership for Year 3 General Practice placements, supporting students and supervisors while delivering high-quality teaching through lectures, tutorials and workshops. Build and strengthen relationships with general practices across the community, expanding placement opportunities and contributing to medical education and General Practice research initiatives. Part time (0.2), ongoing position located in Hobart About the opportunity The Year 3 General Practice Coordinator provides academic and clinical leadership for the delivery and development of General Practice placements within the Hobart Clinical School. Working closely with the Year 3 Unit Coordinator, Clinical School Director and administrative team, the role coordinates placements, supports students and GP supervisors, and ensures high-quality learning experiences. The position focuses on building and maintaining strong partnerships with general practices to expand placement capacity, while also contributing to teaching, research activities, and the development of Tasmania's future medical workforce. This role is ideally suited to an experienced General Practitioner with a passion for medical education, strong organisational and interpersonal skills, and the ability to work autonomously. What you’ll do: Deliver high quality and effectively coordinate Year 3 General Practice rotation, working closely with academic and administrative colleagues to ensure placements, timetables, teaching and assessment activities are planned, communicated and delivered within required timeframes. Build and sustain general practice clinical placement capacity by developing productive relationships with practice principals, owners, managers and supervisors as well as engaging new teaching practices and supporting the ongoing participation and retention of existing placement providers. Ensure a high-quality student and supervisor experience through proactive engagement with students and practices, timely management of concerns or placement issues, appropriate escalation where required and continuous improvement informed by feedback and evaluation. Contribute scholarly, high- quality teaching, research and assessment within the Medicine Program and work collaboratively across the Tasmanian School of Medicine to support consistency, quality and continuous improvement in general practice education. Support the development and delivery of MD research projects in general practice and primary care including supervision or co-supervision of students, identifying suitable projects and supervisors and fostering research collaborations between students and academics. ­ What we’re looking for: A recognised primary medical qualification and current registration as a Medical Practitioner with the Medical Board of Australia as well as substantial clinical experience in general practice. Fellowship of the Royal Australian College of General Practitioners (RACGP) or Australian College of Rural and Remote Medicine (ACRRM), or equivalent relevant experience and qualifications. Demonstrated experience in medical education, clinical teaching and/or supervision of medical students or trainee doctors with an understanding of workplace-based clinical learning and assessment. Excellent interpersonal, communication and problem-solving skills, with demonstrated capacity to support students, supervisors, manage sensitive or complex placement matters and exercise appropriate judgement regarding escalation. A record of contributing to productive links within the medical profession, health services and/or wider community with the capacity to represent the University of Tasmania effectively in external professional settings. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level C and will have a total remuneration package of up to $184,076 comprising base salary within the range of $138,536 to $157,330 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the Roles Specific Skills, Knowledge and Experience. For further information about this position, please contact Lester Pepingco, Director Hobart Clinical School, Lester.Pepingco@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - Year 3 Coordinator of General Practice Placements.pdf . Applications close Wednesday, 23 September 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Year 3 Coordinator of General Practice Placements Hobart 23 Sep 2026 Shape the future of medical education in Tasmania by leading impactful General Practice placements, building strong community partnerships, and inspiring the next generation of doctors. Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Year 3 Coordinator of General Practice Placements Hobart 23 Sep 2026 Shape the future of medical education in Tasmania by leading impactful General Practice placements, building strong community partnerships, and inspiring the next generation of doctors. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/503201/postdoctoral-researcher-underwater-vehicle-hydrodynamics-maneuvering-and-resistance": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Researcher, Underwater Vehicle Hydrodynamics Maneuvering and Resistance",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Melbourne 1 Categories Academic Research Focused 1 Post-doctoral Researcher, Underwater Vehicle Hydrodynamics Maneuvering and Resistance Apply now Job no: 503201 Work type: Full time Location: Melbourne Categories: Research Focused The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Deliver CFD simulations to analyse underwater vehicle performance Access to world-class facilities at DSTG Fishermans Bend and the Australian Maritime College Full time and Fixed-Term (June 2028) based in Victoria with regular travel to Tasmania About the opportunity Join the University of Tasmania as a Postdoctoral Researcher within the Australian Maritime College (AMC), a specialist institute and Australia’s national centre for maritime training, education and research. Within AMC, the Centre for Maritime Engineering and Hydrodynamics (CMEH) delivers specialist programs in Naval Architecture and Ocean Engineering, along with higher degrees by research. CMEH is home to internationally renowned research infrastructure, including the Towing Tank and Model Test Basin, enabling world-class experimental hydrodynamics research. CMEH maintains a long-standing collaboration with the Defence Science and Technology Group (DSTG) investigating marine vehicle hydrodynamics. As part of an expanded research program with DSTG, this role will contribute to studies focused on underwater vehicle manoeuvring and resistance. In this position, you will undertake advanced Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations, primarily based at DSTG Fishermans Bend in Melbourne. You will also spend periods at AMC’s Newnham campus in Launceston supporting physical model-scale experiments in the Towing Tank/Model Test Basin and contributing to the supervision of undergraduate and postgraduate students. This role provides an excellent opportunity for recent PhD graduates and early career researchers to further develop their expertise while working across nationally and internationally recognised research environments. What you’ll do: Make an effective and sustained contribution to the University in achieving its strategic objectives and fulfilling its operational responsibilities. Undertake high-quality research in underwater vehicle manoeuvring and resistance to meet the requirements, outcomes, and timelines stipulated in relevant DSTG/AMC research agreements. Contribute to the development and maintenance of productive and effective links inside the University and locally, nationally and internationally within the discipline, relevant interdisciplinary domains, profession, industry and/or wider community. Work cooperatively with academic and professional staff on research, technical and administrative tasks. ­ What we’re looking for: A PhD in a relevant science or engineering discipline. A demonstrated understanding of, commitment to, and enthusiasm for, research in the field of fluid dynamics. A good record of, and continuing commitment to, research that has achieved national recognition. Worthwhile contributions to the field of hydrodynamics/aerodynamics as demonstrated by a record of quality publications and presentations at conferences. Demonstrated ability to perform CFD simulations to investigate hydrodynamics/aerodynamics. Ability to work independently, show initiative and work effectively as part of a team to conduct and report on scientific research. Demonstrated highly developed written and oral communication skills and interpersonal skills for a variety of audiences. Excellent organisational skills including the ability to prioritise, problem solve, record actions and meet deadlines with the capability of making sound independent decisions when required. An Australian citizen and able to obtain and maintain a personal security clearance of Negative Vetting Level 1. Desirable Role Specific Skills, Knowledge and Experience Demonstrated ability to perform physical model scale experiments to investigate marine vehicle hydrodynamics. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level B and will have a total remuneration package of up to $155,885 comprising base salary within the range of $114,766 to $133,235 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide the following supporting documentation: Resume Cover letter outlining your suitability and motivation for the role Your responses to the essential and desirable role specific skills, knowledge and experience sections from the Position Description below For further information about this position, please contact Associate Professor Jonathan Duffy, Sciences and Engineering, J.Duffy@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - Post-Doctoral Researcher (503201).pdf Applications close Sunday, 27 September 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to be an Australian citizen and able to obtain and maintain a personal security clearance of Negative Vetting Level 1. Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Post-doctoral Researcher, Underwater Vehicle Hydrodynamics Maneuvering and Resistance Melbourne 27 Sep 2026 Shape the future of underwater vehicle design in this unique postdoctoral opportunity combining advanced CFD modelling, hands-on experimentation, and collaboration with national defence researchers Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Post-doctoral Researcher, Underwater Vehicle Hydrodynamics Maneuvering and Resistance Melbourne 27 Sep 2026 Shape the future of underwater vehicle design in this unique postdoctoral opportunity combining advanced CFD modelling, hands-on experimentation, and collaboration with national defence researchers Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
