    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504214/technical-officer": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Professional Laboratory & Research Support 1 Technical Officer Apply now Job no: 504214 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart Categories: Laboratory & Research Support The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Support teaching, research, fieldwork and laboratory operations Join a collaborative team and enjoy excellent employee benefits Full-time, ongoing position based in Hobart About the opportunity The Technical Officer will work within the School of Natural Sciences (SNS), primarily within the Field and Fleet area, but will also provide cross-disciplinary technical support across all areas of the School including undergraduate teaching laboratories, research laboratories, and other research facilities, to ensure smooth and safe operations that support the School’s teaching and research objectives. The School of Natural Sciences trains the next generation of leaders in science, technology, and mathematics—disciplines that underpin Australia’s future capability and innovation. The Technical Officer will assist the Operations Manager and Senior Field and Fleet Officer in managing the day-to-day operations of the School, ensuring the effective and safe delivery of teaching and research activities. The role provides operational, teaching, and training support to staff and students while maintaining compliance with relevant UTAS policies, statutory Work Health and Safety (WHS) regulations, and codes of practice. To be successful in this role, it is essential to demonstrate flexibility, strong interpersonal skills, and an approachable manner. This position requires working collaboratively across a broad technical team and being open to receiving and undertaking tasks in a flexible and responsive manner from various leaders. This is a diverse and dynamic role, offering the opportunity to contribute across a wide range of UTAS facilities and environments. What you’ll do: Support day-to-day field and fleet operations by ensuring the safe, efficient, and compliant use of field equipment and assets. Assist other School Technical Officers with the planning, preparation, and delivery of undergraduate field trips and laboratory classes, including setup and pack-down during peak teaching periods. Maintain field stores and associated assets, ensuring equipment is well-organised, serviceable, and properly maintained. Provide user support for PoolCar and related field or asset management software, assisting staff and students with access and use. Contribute to fieldwork and laboratory safety management by assisting in the review of risk assessments. Support WHS compliance activities, including testing and tagging, workplace inspections, inductions, and the review and maintenance of Safe Work Procedures. Assist in the coordination of laboratory operations, supporting the safe and efficient management of teaching and research laboratories. ­ What we’re looking for: Demonstrated experience in fieldwork, project planning, and risk assessments. Strong hazard identification and risk mitigation skills, with demonstrated experience in Safety, Health and Wellbeing (SHW) processes and the implementation of safe systems of work, including the safe management and handling of chemicals and laboratory hazards. Experience maintaining and coordinating a diverse range of assets, such as laboratory and/or field equipment. Demonstrated ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively within a team environment. Ability to positively interact with students, staff, senior managers and stakeholders external to the University. Well-developed organisational skills with the ability to use initiative, manage competing priorities, and meet deadlines in a dynamic environment. Excellent verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to engage effectively with a diverse range of stakeholders including students, staff, senior managers, and external partners. Demonstrated ability to prepare accurate documentation and maintain detailed records, with a strong focus on quality and compliance. Proficient in the use of a range of computer applications including word processing, spreadsheets, databases, email, and cloud-based systems Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 5 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $105,665 comprising base salary within the range of $80,869 to $90,312 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the success profile. For further information about this position, please contact Tahlia Thomas, Senior Field & Fleet Officer, Tahlia.Thomas@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - Technical Officer - 504214.pdf Applications close Tuesday, 22 September 2026, 11.55pm. Please note: Interviews are expected to be held on 30 September 2026. Candidates are encouraged to keep this date available. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 22 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Technical Officer Hobart 22 Sep 2026 Play a key role in supporting teaching, research and scientific discovery across the School of Natural Sciences Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Technical Officer Hobart 22 Sep 2026 Play a key role in supporting teaching, research and scientific discovery across the School of Natural Sciences Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
